    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d814">

                <pb facs="1004" n="994" sameAs="frus:frus1947v07/pg994"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00115 PW/5–2047: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Stuart</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-05-20T16:00:00+09:00">May 20 [<hi
                                rendition="#italic">10</hi>] 1947—4 [<hi rendition="#italic">6</hi>]
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received May 20—2:20 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1016. Following is repeat of message sent by Army to Tokyo reported missing by
                    Washington: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>Following is Mukden’s 92, May 2 (delayed in transmission to Embassy):</p>
                        <p>“Chinese authorities have told local Japs that Jap repatriation will be
                            resumed shortly and will involve repatriation of half (15,000) of the
                            30,000 Japanese (technicians and 20,000 dependents thereof) still in
                            National Govt areas Manchuria (including 10,000 Mukden and 6,000
                            Changchun).</p>
                        <p>“A high type Jap, who is a leader of local Jap community and prominent
                            technical advisor, informs us that Japs fear Chinese lack facilities
                            (ships, etc.) and concrete plans needed for fulfillment this offer, and
                            that without American aid there will be uncertainties and delays <pb
                                facs="1005" n="995" xml:id="pg995"/> which will entail great
                            difficulties and expense for Japs who must make preparations for
                            repatriation. Jap community anxious to know when first ship will arrive
                            Hulutao, whether American help will be provided, and what type ship will
                            be used.</p>
                        <p>“Above transmitted for information Embassy, and any American organ
                            assisting Jap repatriation. Ward.”<anchor n="11" xml:id="d814-f1"/>
                        </p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>Executive Office of Embassy investigating.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stuart</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d814-f1" n="11" xml:id="d814-f1-content">
                <p>Angus Ward, Consul General at Mukden.</p>
